// Unstructured Jacobi XPBD for the distilled (HOOD/TinyHOOD) cloth path.
//
// This is the port of real_scene/xpbd.py::_apply_fused plus the two passes that turn it into a
// soft-guided multirate solver: `_apply_area` and `_resolve_guide`. Read those functions alongside
// this file; the arithmetic is deliberately line-for-line the same, and tests/test_xpbd.py pins the
// Python side down against its own two-pass Jacobi reference.
//
// Why one *subgroup* per vertex rather than one thread
// ---------------------------------------------------
// The natural Jacobi sweep needs two dispatches per iteration -- one over constraints to produce
// every delta lambda, then one over vertices to gather them -- because a vertex cannot see a
// neighbouring constraint's result until the constraint pass has finished writing. XPBD on this
// hardware is dispatch-bound, not work-bound, so each vertex here recomputes the multiplier update
// of every constraint it touches and the iteration stays a single dispatch.
//
// The first version of that ran one *thread* per vertex, which made CH10032's 1377 vertices into
// (1377 + 127) / 128 = 11 workgroups on a 34-SM part. Measured cost was 9.44 us per iteration
// against a 2.8 us estimate, and the diagnosis was clean: hood_grid64 has 2.31x the slot work and
// runs 1.40x *faster* per iteration, which rules out both work and bandwidth and leaves occupancy.
//
// So a vertex now gets 32 lanes and a workgroup carries four of them, which is about 345 workgroups
// on CH10032. 32 rather than the whole 128: the slot width here is 18, so 32 lanes already cover a
// vertex in one pass, and the remaining 96 lanes of a 128-wide-per-vertex layout would only add
// reduction depth. hood_world_nearest.comp is the precedent for the shape ("The work was never the
// problem; the occupancy was"), with the one-vertex-per-128-lane-group choice narrowed here because
// its inner loop was 4096 proxies deep and this one is 18 slots deep.
//
// Consequences the reader has to know about
// -----------------------------------------
// * `lambda` is per (vertex, slot), so both endpoints of a constraint keep their own copy, and those
//   copies must not drift. Three things guarantee they do not, and none of them is affected by the
//   lane layout, because each lane owns whole slots and never splits one:
//     - both threads form pairs[c].x minus pairs[c].y in that stored order, never "mine minus
//       theirs", so the difference and its length are the same floats on both;
//     - weightSum is read from the baked asset rather than recomputed, because w_a + w_b on one
//       thread against w_b + w_a on the other is not guaranteed to give the same float (see
//       real_scene/xpbd.py::SolverTables and tools/bake_xpbd_constraints.py);
//     - the endpoint-dependent sign is applied to the correction after the multiplier update, never
//       inside it.
//   Lambda is therefore bit-identical to the one-thread-per-vertex version.
// * The *correction sum* is not. A fixed-order groupshared tree over 32 lanes adds the same numbers
//   in a different sequence than a serial loop over 18 slots, so the last bits of a position move.
//   Measured, that shift is far smaller than expected and the Python-versus-Vulkan gate did not have
//   to move: results/xpbd128_verify.json reports max 1.683965e-3 for this shape against 1.683891e-3
//   for one-thread-per-vertex, a difference of 7e-8. The reason is that the 1.68e-3 is not a
//   summation artifact at all -- it is the one-sided `max(residual, 0)` and the contact's
//   `signed < 0` having their fixed point exactly on a branch boundary, with about 90 constraints and
//   330 contacts per step sitting within 1e-7 of it, so a few vertices take different sides on CPU
//   and GPU. Reordering an 18-term sum perturbs the result three to four orders below that.
//   Steps 1-7 of that same run are 1.19e-7 to 9.02e-7, which is where the reordering actually shows.
// * There is no early `return`. With four vertices per group the tail group has subgroups whose
//   vertex is out of range, and a pinned vertex used to return before the reduction; either would
//   leave a thread absent from a GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync the others still execute. Inactive
//   lanes contribute zero and fall through instead.
//
// Jacobi also means every vertex must read the SAME positions, so the runtime ping-pongs between
// clothPosition and a scratch buffer rather than updating in place. Reading and writing one buffer
// would silently turn this into a nondeterministic partial Gauss-Seidel.
//
// Constraint targets are baked calibrated lengths, NOT rest lengths. Skinning the authored rest mesh
// into frame 0 already puts 7.7-8.2% of CH10032's edges past 1.5x rest (p95 1.890 on tpose,
// 2.025 on sprint), so aiming at cloth_rest would contract those edges by up to 12x. See
// plans/gnn/gnn-xpbd-v2.md section 1.4.

struct Push {
    uint clothCount;
    uint constraintCount;
    uint slotWidth;
    uint flags;              // bit 0 one-sided, bit 1 collision, bit 2 guide, bit 3 area
    float timestep;
    float relaxation;
    float contactOffset;
    float stretchCompliance;
    float bendCompliance;
    float guideCompliance;
    float areaFloor;
    float areaCompliance;
    uint triangleCount;
    // 0 = distance sweep, 1 = area floor. Uniform across the dispatch, so the barriers below stay
    // group-synchronous. Two dispatches only when the area floor is on: the area pass has to read
    // positions the distance sweep has finished writing everywhere, which is a barrier, while the
    // guide and the contact are per-vertex and fold into whichever pass writes last.
    uint pass;
};
[[vk::push_constant]] Push push;

static const uint XPBD_ONE_SIDED = 1u;
static const uint XPBD_COLLISION = 2u;
static const uint XPBD_GUIDE = 4u;
static const uint XPBD_AREA = 8u;
static const uint XPBD_KIND_BEND = 1u;

static const uint XPBD_PASS_DISTANCE = 0u;
static const uint XPBD_PASS_AREA = 1u;

// One vertex per 32 lanes, four vertices per 128-thread group. See the header.
static const uint XPBD_LANES = 32u;
static const uint XPBD_VERTICES_PER_GROUP = 4u;

[[vk::binding(0)]] StructuredBuffer<uint2> xpbdPairs;
[[vk::binding(1)]] StructuredBuffer<float> xpbdTargetLength;
[[vk::binding(2)]] StructuredBuffer<float> xpbdWeightSum;
[[vk::binding(3)]] StructuredBuffer<uint> xpbdKind;
[[vk::binding(4)]] StructuredBuffer<uint> xpbdSlots;
[[vk::binding(5)]] StructuredBuffer<float> xpbdSigns;
[[vk::binding(6)]] StructuredBuffer<float> xpbdIncident;
[[vk::binding(7)]] StructuredBuffer<float> xpbdInverseMass;
[[vk::binding(8)]] RWStructuredBuffer<float> xpbdLambda;
[[vk::binding(9)]] StructuredBuffer<float4> positionIn;
[[vk::binding(10)]] RWStructuredBuffer<float4> positionOut;
[[vk::binding(11)]] StructuredBuffer<uint> worldObstacle;
[[vk::binding(12)]] StructuredBuffer<float4> proxyTarget;
[[vk::binding(13)]] StructuredBuffer<float4> proxyNormal;
// The network's prediction for this substep, and how far each vertex is trusted to follow it.
[[vk::binding(14)]] StructuredBuffer<float4> guideTarget;
[[vk::binding(15)]] StructuredBuffer<float> guideConfidence;
[[vk::binding(16)]] RWStructuredBuffer<float> guideLambda;
// Triangle topology for the area floor. `clothTriangles` is the flat uint3 list from .vcloth2 and
// the offsets/indices are the per-vertex incident-triangle CSR hoodLoadAssets already derives from
// it for hood_features.comp -- no new baked section, only `areaTarget`.
[[vk::binding(17)]] StructuredBuffer<uint> clothTriangles;
[[vk::binding(18)]] StructuredBuffer<uint> clothTriangleOffsets;
[[vk::binding(19)]] StructuredBuffer<uint> clothTriangleIndices;
[[vk::binding(20)]] StructuredBuffer<float> areaTarget;
[[vk::binding(21)]] RWStructuredBuffer<float> areaLambda;

groupshared float3 xpbdLaneCorrection[XPBD_LANES * XPBD_VERTICES_PER_GROUP];

// Fixed-order tree reduction inside one vertex's 32 lanes. The order is a property of the lane
// index, not of scheduling, so the result is reproducible on any vendor and needs no float atomic.
// Every lane of the workgroup reaches every barrier, including the lanes whose vertex does not exist.
float3 xpbdReduceLanes(uint base, uint lane)
{
    [unroll] for (uint stride = XPBD_LANES / 2u; stride > 0u; stride >>= 1) {
        if (lane < stride) {
            xpbdLaneCorrection[base + lane] += xpbdLaneCorrection[base + lane + stride];
        }
        GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync();
    }
    return xpbdLaneCorrection[base];
}

[numthreads(128, 1, 1)]
void main(uint3 groupId : SV_GroupID, uint threadIndex : SV_GroupIndex)
{
    const uint subgroup = threadIndex / XPBD_LANES;
    const uint lane = threadIndex % XPBD_LANES;
    const uint vertex = groupId.x * XPBD_VERTICES_PER_GROUP + subgroup;
    const uint base = subgroup * XPBD_LANES;

    const bool exists = vertex < push.clothCount;
    const float inverseMass = exists ? xpbdInverseMass[vertex] : 0.0;
    // Pinned. Zero inverse mass already makes the correction zero, but skipping the lambda writes as
    // well matches `torch.where(pinned, 0, correction)` in the reference and keeps the pin an exact
    // infinite-mass anchor.
    const bool movable = exists && inverseMass > 0.0;
    const float3 position = exists ? positionIn[vertex].xyz : float3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);

    const float inverseSquaredStep = 1.0 / max(push.timestep * push.timestep, 1.0e-12);
    float3 correction = float3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);

    if (push.pass == XPBD_PASS_DISTANCE && movable) {
        const bool oneSided = (push.flags & XPBD_ONE_SIDED) != 0u;
        // Lane `l` owns slots l, l + 32, l + 64, ... so 18 slots fit in one trip with 14 lanes idle.
        for (uint slot = lane; slot < push.slotWidth; slot += XPBD_LANES) {
            const uint entry = vertex * push.slotWidth + slot;
            const uint constraint = xpbdSlots[entry];
            // Unused lanes hold the sentinel index `constraintCount`.
            if (constraint >= push.constraintCount) continue;

            const uint2 pair = xpbdPairs[constraint];
            const float3 delta = positionIn[pair.x].xyz - positionIn[pair.y].xyz;
            const float distance = length(delta);
            if (!(distance > 1.0e-9)) continue;

            const float compliance = xpbdKind[constraint] == XPBD_KIND_BEND
                ? push.bendCompliance : push.stretchCompliance;
            const float alpha = compliance * inverseSquaredStep;
            const float weightSum = xpbdWeightSum[constraint];
            if (!(weightSum > 0.0)) continue;   // both endpoints pinned

            float residual = distance - xpbdTargetLength[constraint];
            if (oneSided) residual = max(residual, 0.0);

            const float lambda = xpbdLambda[entry];
            const float step = (-residual - alpha * lambda) / max(weightSum + alpha, 1.0e-20);
            xpbdLambda[entry] = lambda + step;
            correction += xpbdSigns[entry] * (delta / distance) * step;
        }
    } else if (push.pass == XPBD_PASS_AREA && movable) {
        // One-sided triangle area floor, `C = max(areaFloor * A_0 - A(x), 0)`. The existing structural
        // set is mesh-edge distances plus a two-hop distance standing in for bending, run one-sided,
        // and nothing in it objects to a triangle collapsing: a short edge is legal and zero area is
        // legal. Measured on sprint_start step 120 the hybrid holds the best edge p95 of the three
        // branches (1.291) together with the most collapsed edges (13.3%), the smallest triangles
        // (area median 0.478) and the most flipped ones (0.435).
        const float alpha = push.areaCompliance * inverseSquaredStep;
        const uint begin = clothTriangleOffsets[vertex];
        const uint end = clothTriangleOffsets[vertex + 1];
        for (uint slot = begin + lane; slot < end; slot += XPBD_LANES) {
            const uint face = clothTriangleIndices[slot];   // `triangle` is an HLSL keyword
            if (face >= push.triangleCount) continue;
            const uint3 corner = uint3(clothTriangles[face * 3 + 0],
                                       clothTriangles[face * 3 + 1],
                                       clothTriangles[face * 3 + 2]);
            // Stored winding order, so all three vertices of the triangle form the same normal from
            // the same floats -- the ternary analogue of "pairs[c].x minus pairs[c].y".
            const float3 first = positionIn[corner.x].xyz;
            const float3 second = positionIn[corner.y].xyz;
            const float3 third = positionIn[corner.z].xyz;
            const float3 normal = cross(second - first, third - first);
            const float twiceArea = length(normal);
            if (!(twiceArea > 1.0e-12)) continue;
            const float3 unit = normal / twiceArea;

            const float residual = max(push.areaFloor * areaTarget[face] - 0.5 * twiceArea, 0.0);
            // grad_i A for corners 0, 1, 2. They sum to zero, as a constraint that cannot move the
            // centre of mass must.
            const float3 gradient0 = 0.5 * cross(second - third, unit);
            const float3 gradient1 = 0.5 * cross(third - first, unit);
            const float3 gradient2 = 0.5 * cross(first - second, unit);
            // Unlike a distance constraint the gradients are not unit vectors, so the denominator is
            // sum_i w_i |grad_i A|^2 and not sum_i w_i -- state-dependent, so there is nothing to
            // bake. The three threads agree by summing in corner order 0, 1, 2, which is fixed by
            // the mesh rather than by which vertex is asking.
            const float denominator =
                  xpbdInverseMass[corner.x] * dot(gradient0, gradient0)
                + xpbdInverseMass[corner.y] * dot(gradient1, gradient1)
                + xpbdInverseMass[corner.z] * dot(gradient2, gradient2);
            if (!(denominator > 0.0)) continue;

            const float lambda = areaLambda[slot];
            const float step = (-residual - alpha * lambda) / max(denominator + alpha, 1.0e-20);
            areaLambda[slot] = lambda + step;
            // grad C = -grad A while the floor is violated, so this inflates the triangle. Each
            // vertex applies only the gradient of the corner it occupies.
            const float3 own = vertex == corner.x ? gradient0
                : (vertex == corner.y ? gradient1 : gradient2);
            correction -= own * step;
        }
    }

    xpbdLaneCorrection[base + lane] = correction;
    GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync();
    const float3 total = xpbdReduceLanes(base, lane);

    if (lane != 0u || !exists) return;

    const float averaging = push.pass == XPBD_PASS_DISTANCE
        ? max(xpbdIncident[vertex], 1.0)
        : max(float(clothTriangleOffsets[vertex + 1] - clothTriangleOffsets[vertex]), 1.0);
    float3 updated = position + push.relaxation * inverseMass * total / averaging;

    // Everything below is per-vertex: it reads only this vertex's own state, so it needs no barrier
    // and folds into whichever pass writes the position out.
    const bool lastPass = push.pass == XPBD_PASS_AREA || (push.flags & XPBD_AREA) == 0u;
    if (lastPass && (push.flags & XPBD_GUIDE) != 0u && movable) {
        // Compliant pull towards the network's prediction. `C = |x - g|` is one constraint on one
        // vertex, so grad C is a unit vector and grad C M^-1 grad C^T is just w:
        //
        //     Delta lambda = (-C - alpha * lambda) / (w + alpha),   Delta x = w * n * Delta lambda
        //
        // `n` points from the guide towards the vertex and Delta lambda is negative, so the vertex
        // moves towards the guide, closing w / (w + alpha) of the gap on the first iteration. That
        // fraction is the dial the whole scheme exists for: alpha = 0 snaps onto the prediction,
        // alpha -> infinity leaves the vertex wherever the physics put it. Accumulating lambda is what
        // makes repeated application settle at the compliant equilibrium instead of stiffening into a
        // hard guide -- see real_scene/xpbd.py::_resolve_guide, and note this is NOT the multiplier
        // warm start gate G0 measured as worthless: that failed because a displacement in the null
        // space of J^T cannot be written as J^T lambda, and this constraint's Jacobian is the identity.
        //
        // Applied outside the incident average above on purpose. Folding the guide into the
        // structural slots would divide every constraint at this vertex by 19 instead of 18 and put a
        // stiff guide in a tug of war with stiff edges under Jacobi averaging.
        const float confidence = guideConfidence[vertex];
        if (confidence > 0.0) {
            const float3 delta = updated - guideTarget[vertex].xyz;
            const float distance = length(delta);
            if (distance > 1.0e-9) {
                const float alpha = push.guideCompliance * inverseSquaredStep / confidence;
                const float lambda = guideLambda[vertex];
                const float step = (-distance - alpha * lambda) / max(inverseMass + alpha, 1.0e-20);
                guideLambda[vertex] = lambda + step;
                updated += inverseMass * (delta / distance) * step;
            }
        }
    }

    // One-sided plane projection against the nearest body proxy, the same contact the network saw.
    // Each cloth vertex has at most one, so this needs no accumulation. It comes after the guide so
    // non-penetration is never traded away for staying near the prediction. Pinned vertices are
    // excluded, matching `movable` in real_scene/xpbd.py::_resolve_contacts and the early return the
    // one-thread-per-vertex version used to take.
    if (lastPass && (push.flags & XPBD_COLLISION) != 0u && movable) {
        const uint obstacle = worldObstacle[vertex];
        if (obstacle != 0xffffffffu) {
            const float3 normal = normalize(proxyNormal[obstacle].xyz);
            const float signedDistance = dot(updated - proxyTarget[obstacle].xyz, normal) - push.contactOffset;
            if (signedDistance < 0.0) updated -= signedDistance * normal;
        }
    }

    positionOut[vertex] = float4(all(isfinite(updated)) ? updated : position, 1.0);
}
